From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ieee1905: send unicast CMDUs to non-adjacent AL addresses

i1905_do_cmdu_tx() can only reach a direct 1905 neighbour. It tries the
neighbour cache, then the txpaths, and both are populated from direct links
only, so on a chained mesh every node past the first hop is unreachable --
even though the L2 path exists and carries data at full rate.

Measured on the controller of a four-node mesh 2026-09-01:

  send_cmdu: cmdu 8004 to <depth-2 node> was NOT sent (mid 0)
             - destination is not a direct 1905 neighbour

0x8004 refused 15 times in 30 s to each of the two depth-2 agents, 0x0008
6 times, not one success. The mesh stayed up only because agents report
upwards on their own; nothing the controller sends downwards arrives.

The bridge fallback added by 0006 never ran. ieee1905d puts bridge *ports*
into dm.self.iflist, not the bridge itself -- i1905.c:1746-1790 sends the
bridge to dm.self.miflist and br_get_iflist() splits it into ports, and
i1905_setup_interface() (i1905.c:1553) refuses bridges outright. So
if_isbridge() is false for every entry and the loop always falls through.
Runtime confirms it: 13 interfaces, br-lan not among them. Its measurement
note cannot be reproduced against this tree either, so both go.

What does know the far nodes is iface->nbriflist, filled from received
topology responses, which carries the AL address of every device seen behind
an interface with a direct flag rather than only the adjacent one:

  ap-mld-2.sta1  nbr_ieee1905id 0e:ed:8f:c2:0f:83  direct=false

ieee1905_send_cmdu() (i1905_extension.c:557-580) already sends this way and
asks neither the neighbour cache nor the txpaths, so the two entry points
disagreed about what is reachable. Mirror that walk here.

Verified by hand on hardware before writing this, same tree:

  ubus call ieee1905 cmdu ... "dst":"c2:c9:79:f6:bc:9e"
    -> {"status":"fail"}
  the same call plus "ifname":"ap-mld-2.sta1"
    -> {"status":"ok","mid":57322}

ap-mld-2.sta1 is the WDS netdev towards the neighbour; it is in iflist and it
is not excluded, because exclude_ifname matches on equal length and
ap-mld-2 != ap-mld-2.sta1.

One peer can appear under several interfaces and an entry goes stale before
its ageout timer removes it, so the walk takes the most recently refreshed
match rather than the first one -- the same hazard 0005 hit on the receive
side. Excluded interfaces and entries flagged invalid are skipped.

No new socket and no new SA: the frame still leaves through an existing
interface with that port's MAC, exactly as a direct-neighbour CMDU does.

--- a/src/i1905_cmdresp.c
+++ b/src/i1905_cmdresp.c
@@ -1075,59 +1075,85 @@
 			}
 		}
 
-		/* Last resort: hand it to the bridge.
+		/* Not a direct neighbour: ask the interface link tables.
 		 *
-		 * Neither the neighbour cache nor the txpaths know this AL MAC, and
-		 * up to now that meant the CMDU was dropped. But 1905.1 defines no
-		 * forwarding for unicast at all - Table 6-4 says a received unicast
-		 * CMDU is processed and "not transmitted on any of its interfaces" -
-		 * and the forwarding entity is explicitly out of scope, required only
-		 * to "be interoperable with IEEE 802.1 bridging". A unicast CMDU to a
-		 * device several hops away is therefore expected to be delivered by
-		 * the bridge, not routed by us. Refusing to send it is our own
-		 * addition, and it is the one that fails.
-		 *
-		 * The neighbour cache only ever learns direct neighbours, so on a
-		 * chained mesh every node beyond the first is unreachable by this
-		 * path even though the L2 route exists and carries data at full rate.
-		 *
-		 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-31, mesh formed as
-		 * controller <- agentA <- agentB:
-		 *
-		 *   ubus call ieee1905 cmdu '{"type":2,"dst":"<agentB al-mac>"}'
-		 *     -> {"status":"fail"}                      (this branch)
-		 *   same call with "ifname":"br-lan"
-		 *     -> {"status":"ok","mid":19391}
-		 *     -> tcpdump on agentB: 3 frames with DA = its AL MAC arrived,
-		 *        3x type 0x0002 Topology Query in, 3x 0x0003 Response out,
-		 *        controller went from num_nodes 2 to 3.
-		 *
-		 * So the bridge delivers it. The destination AL MAC is not in the
-		 * forwarding database - it belongs to no netdev of ours - and an
-		 * unknown unicast destination is flooded, which reaches the far node
-		 * through the intermediary. That is also why this cannot loop: the
-		 * bridge applies its own split horizon, and a CMDU that does arrive
-		 * is processed and never retransmitted.
-		 *
-		 * Other open 1905 stacks do this unconditionally - no
-		 * neighbour lookup on the transmit path at all - and the
-		 * Broadband Forum reference implementation assumes it.
+		 * The neighbour cache and the txpaths only ever learn direct
+		 * neighbours, so on a chained mesh every node past the first hop
+		 * is unreachable from here even though the L2 path exists and
+		 * carries data at full rate. Measured on the controller of a
+		 * four-node mesh 2026-09-01: cmdu 0x8004 to each of the two
+		 * depth-2 agents was refused 15 times in 30 s with "destination
+		 * is not a direct 1905 neighbour", and not one frame left the
+		 * box. The mesh held together only because agents report upwards
+		 * on their own.
+		 *
+		 * iface->nbriflist does know them. It is filled from received
+		 * topology responses and carries the AL address of every device
+		 * seen behind that interface, direct or not:
+		 *
+		 *   ap-mld-2.sta1  nbr_ieee1905id 0e:ed:8f:c2:0f:83  direct=false
+		 *
+		 * ieee1905_send_cmdu() in i1905_extension.c already sends this
+		 * way and consults neither the neighbour cache nor the txpaths.
+		 * This mirrors that walk so both entry points behave alike.
+		 *
+		 * Checked by hand on hardware before writing this, same tree:
+		 *
+		 *   ubus call ieee1905 cmdu with {"type":2,"dst":"c2:c9:.."}
+		 *     -> {"status":"fail"}
+		 *   the same call plus "ifname":"ap-mld-2.sta1"
+		 *     -> {"status":"ok","mid":57322}
+		 *
+		 * One peer can appear under several interfaces, and an entry
+		 * goes stale before its ageout timer removes it, so take the
+		 * most recently refreshed match rather than the first one --
+		 * the same hazard patch 0005 hit on the receive side.
 		 */
 		if (ret) {
+			struct i1905_neighbor_interface *nif = NULL;
+			struct i1905_interface *best = NULL;
+			time_t best_tsp = 0;
+
 			list_for_each_entry(iface, &priv->dm.self.iflist, list) {
-				if (iface->exclude || !if_isbridge(iface->ifname))
+				if (iface->exclude)
 					continue;
 
-				ret = i1905_cmdu_tx(iface->priv, vid, dst, src,
+				list_for_each_entry(nif, &iface->nbriflist, list) {
+					if (nif->invalid)
+						continue;
+
+					if (!hwaddr_equal(nif->aladdr, dst))
+						continue;
+
+					if (!best || nif->tsp > best_tsp) {
+						best = iface;
+						best_tsp = nif->tsp;
+					}
+				}
+			}
+
+			if (best) {
+				ret = i1905_cmdu_tx(best->priv, vid, dst, src,
 						    type, &mid, data, data_len,
 						    true);
 				if (!ret) {
 					i1905_warn(LOG_TX,
-						   "%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT " handed to bridge %s\n",
-						  __func__, type, MAC2STR(dst),
-						  iface->ifname);
+						   "%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT " sent via %s (not a direct neighbour)\n",
+						   __func__, type, MAC2STR(dst),
+						   best->ifname);
 					goto done;
 				}
+
+				i1905_warn(LOG_TX, "%s: cmdu tx failed\n", __func__);
 			}
+
+			/* Say so when nothing matched. Without this the branch is
+			 * silent on a miss and "no message" cannot be told apart
+			 * from "never ran" - which is exactly where we stood on
+			 * 2026-09-02 after watching 58 refusals go past. */
+			if (!best)
+				i1905_warn(LOG_TX,
+					   "%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT ": no interface knows this AL address\n",
+					   __func__, type, MAC2STR(dst));
 		}
 	}
